WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.350
In January of 2021,
Alaska was on the cusp

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.510
of the most highly
anticipated vaccine launch

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.970
in 50 years, the COVID vaccine.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.460
So as the state began
to roll out shots

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.370
to senior citizens
age 65 and above,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.540
the hottest ticket in town was
a COVID-19 vaccine appointment.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.413
So when a batch of
appointments would come online,

00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:29.580
those would be snatched up
within hours or sometimes

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.510
even minutes.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.189
And back in those
days, everybody

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.490
needed an online appointment.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.150
You couldn't just walk in and
get your COVID-19 vaccine.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.600
So it's a really exciting time.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.600
But we found out pretty
quickly that the website

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.850
the state was using to
get people signed up

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.250
was not working well
for senior citizens.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.170
It was hiding the appointments
in several pages of results.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.350
It was made for health
care professionals working

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.078
on desktop computers,
but wasn't working

00:00:51.078 --> 00:00:52.120
for people on the phones.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.080
The maps were distorted.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.497
It's showing the
Sacramento County

00:00:54.497 --> 00:00:57.363
Jail instead of the Anchorage
Health Department here.

00:00:57.363 --> 00:00:59.280
And so we started to
hear from senior citizens

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.420
that they were struggling,
that they were not

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.430
making it through the
appointment registration

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.370
process.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.450
And so at this really
critical juncture,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.503
the senior citizens are
starting to contact us

00:01:08.503 --> 00:01:10.920
and saying that there are some
are at the cusp of actually

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:11.730
giving up.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.850
And at this really important
moment of the pandemic,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.480
senior citizens are frustrated.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.680
The state is frustrated.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.812
And I'm frustrated,
especially when

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.020
I learned that there's no
changes and no improvements

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.580
that can come to the
website anytime soon.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.130
But I knew that
there must be a way

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.440
to improve this
website experience,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.450
and I knew I had
to at least try.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.902
So my name is Ben Matheson.

00:01:31.902 --> 00:01:33.360
I work for the
Anchorage Innovation

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.810
Team in Anchorage, Alaska.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.250
We're a small team inside city
government that uses data,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.350
human centered design and
technology to solve problems.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.500
So we try to improve
the lives of residents,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.200
solve problem with city
departments and save money.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.880
So today I'm going
to tell you about how

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.100
we built a vaccine finder
for the state of Alaska

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.160
in one week using R
and hope to show you

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.380
how R is a great tool
for rapid prototyping

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.860
and rapid building of products.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.770
And after this, I hope
that you can better

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.950
appreciate how you have the
tools and abilities to really

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.820
make some cool custom,
rapid products.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.570
And so before we started
building anything

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.090
we wanted to validate
there was a problem

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.460
and find out exactly
what this problem was.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.680
So we got on the phone
with senior citizens

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.910
as they were trying to
sign up for their vaccines.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.760
And we found out a few things.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:23.090
One is that they are struggling
through a complex website.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.520
We learned that
they're struggling

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.937
with some pretty complicated
health questions that show up

00:02:26.937 --> 00:02:29.050
in these vaccine
registration systems

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.640
and also learning that the adult
children sometimes of seniors

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.390
are the ones who are actually
signing up for appointments.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.150
And so we tried to narrow
the problem a little bit to,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.130
how can we help Anchorage
senior citizens who

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.780
want their vaccine really
quickly find that appointment

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.682
so that they can
get their vaccine?

00:02:46.682 --> 00:02:48.640
And so before we started
building anything big,

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.560
we wanted to do a
no budget build,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.280
an MVP, a minimum
viable product.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.920
So in this case, what is
the smallest, fastest,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.080
quickest thing that we
can make that proves

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.155
that there is value?

00:02:59.155 --> 00:03:00.780
Get it into the hands
of people and see

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.920
whether we're
helping the problem

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.200
or if we need to go in a
totally different direction.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.270
And so this was literally
Friday night project.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.940
It's Friday night
in early January,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.507
and I picked up the
tools that I use

00:03:10.507 --> 00:03:12.340
when I want to make
something fast and easy.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.780
And not easy.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.460
But I picked up dplyr.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.245
I use stringr and
lubridate and we're

00:03:18.245 --> 00:03:20.370
trying to capture vaccine
appointments as they come

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.030
online and as they disappear.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.090
And we learn pretty quickly
that there's no API coming.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.630
There's no programmatic
access to vaccine appointments

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:28.690
in Anchorage.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.160
So it's time to scrape.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.160
So we use rvest to scrape
the vaccine web pages.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.540
And anybody who's ever used
web scraper knows that

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.240
it's like trying to
create order from chaos.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.900
You know, you find a div that
appears to have the information

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:43.960
that you're looking for.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.780
You know, it's border gray
200 is the div that we want.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.740
Here we go.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.190
Grab that div and
extract the text from it.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.400
And from that text
we try to turn that

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.190
into structured data
that serves our needs.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.570
So rip it apart, mutate,
modify, put it back together

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.810
and make structured
data out of this text.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.548
So we're scraping, we're
able to capture that data.

00:04:06.548 --> 00:04:08.340
But then it's time for
a really quick front

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.890
end of how do you discover
vaccine appointments?

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.870
And because this
website was a reaction

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.820
to an overly
complicated website,

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.850
we wanted to go with something
super simple, super fast, super

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:18.579
lightweight.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.010
And so I went with
an HTML table.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.310
So on the left is
the prototype that

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.750
came together after a few
hours on a Friday night.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:30.347
It's a web scraping service
that scrapes every 60 seconds

00:04:30.347 --> 00:04:32.430
to try to find those
appointments as they come up,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.490
as they disappear.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.020
And we show those in a really
simple format no filtering,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.670
no menus, no maps, no anything.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:42.750
It's how do you really quickly
find those actual vaccine

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:43.830
appointments?

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:45.160
And then it's time to test.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.030
So we've got this
very prototype website

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.580
and we sent it to people who
are working on the vaccine call

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.160
center.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.330
It's a team of about 40 people
who spend all day on the phone

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.790
with senior citizens
trying to help them sign up

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:57.700
for appointments.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.400
And they're able to give us
some really good feedback.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.450
They're able to see
if it's helping.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:04.580
And it did help.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.735
They were able to use
it as a power tool.

00:05:06.735 --> 00:05:08.610
This was the vaccine
call center's power tool

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:13.020
that we tried out and then
something else happened

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.120
in that this website
started to be passed around

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.120
between people.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.500
And it's not a real website.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.500
It's not a real product.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.670
But over the course of a
weekend, all of a sudden

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.460
there's hundreds of people
using this website and signing up

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.280
for appointments.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.230
And we're hearing
back and people

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.500
are having a good experience.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.650
So we know that there's
something there,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.480
but it turned into
an unexpected launch.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.540
We've got this product,
this prototype,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.583
but the state liked it.

00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:35.500
They wanted to use it.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.500
And so we developed a
really quick partnership

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.708
with the state health
departments, the Anchorage Health

00:05:39.708 --> 00:05:41.910
Department and the
emergency operation center.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.700
And nobody from Alaska
can do a presentation

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.200
without a map of Alaska.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.135
But I just want to clarify.

00:05:48.135 --> 00:05:49.510
I work for the
City of Anchorage.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.740
So we're the biggest
city in Alaska, about 40%

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.070
of the state's population.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.800
But what the state
wants to do is

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.900
to use this scraping
based vaccine

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.070
finder for the entire state.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.370
So they want to use it
in Fairbanks and Juneau

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:03.850
and Ketchikan and Kodiak.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.978
And I'd say that's awesome.

00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:06.520
That's great.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.460
But we have to scramble here.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.960
And so over the
course of the week,

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.800
we cleaned up the scraper.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.200
We made it real by
buying a domain.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.570
And about one week
later, we launched.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:20.610
So we had an Alaska statewide
vaccine finder tool.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.470
It's what I showed before,
but basically cleaned up.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:27.510
It's appointments that you
can find by location, address,

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.450
vaccine type.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.050
It's text above on the
phone that works well.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:32.750
And that's it.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:34.500
That's that's what we
generated and that's

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.440
what we started using.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.510
And so over the course
of January and February,

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.790
we use this for
things like signing up

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:42.500
people for these
big mass vaccination

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.960
clinics at a basketball
arena or when we launched

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.960
drive-through clinics
at hockey arenas.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.860
We sign people up
through this website.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.040
So that's a lot of fun.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.290
And we got some good feedback.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.110
People, people were able
to use this to connect

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:57.140
to their appointments.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:02.510
And this tool was doing
what we hoped it was doing.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.150
But it wasn't always
straightforward,

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:06.120
it wasn't always easy.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.910
And things definitely don't
always go according to plan.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:12.138
So on March 9th,
2021, we got word

00:07:12.138 --> 00:07:14.430
that the governor was going
to make a big announcement.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.597
Alaska is going to be the
first state in the country

00:07:16.597 --> 00:07:18.450
to go fully open for everybody.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.510
Everybody age 16
and above would be

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:23.030
able to get their
vaccine appointments.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:28.050
So at 4:44 p.m., I had to
send this message to my boss.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.650
The website was down.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.660
We had some weird data
come across the scraper

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.410
at the moment that we
were expecting the biggest

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:34.970
surge in traffic, maybe
the highest amount

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.220
of interest over the course
of the entire rollout.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.340
I have to deal with this, but
I was able to filter it out,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.340
keep it online and was
really happy that we could

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.410
put this banner up
saying everybody aged 16

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.520
and older can now get
their vaccine in Alaska.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.170
So we stayed online throughout
that pretty busy day.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.075
And over the course
of this project,

00:07:54.075 --> 00:07:55.700
we didn't have the
most stable website.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:57.350
We didn't have the
fanciest website.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:07:59.320
We didn't have the
fastest website.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.830
But our measure success
was how fast could we

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.680
adapt to everything,
adapt to new eligibility,

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.230
adapt to new data,
adapt to new pharmacies,

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:08.740
adapt to new requirements.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:12.660
That's exactly
what we had to do.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.440
This this vaccine rollout was
one where Alaska was first

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.040
and we couldn't
copy anybody else.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.060
We couldn't copy California
or New York, or Washington,

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.050
but we kind of had to
make it up as we go.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:24.820
If we could copy, we would.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.930
But we just couldn't.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:30.290
And some projects, the timelines
aren't what you expect.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:33.919
So in early January, we heard
a really optimistic timeline

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:36.600
that vaccines will be open
by the summer solstice.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.350
So approximately a
six month rollout

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:41.890
of those vaccines
before they open up.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.870
But here it was March 9th, and
it's the Iditarod trail sled

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:45.390
dog race.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:48.810
And we've got vaccines
rolling out to everybody.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.950
So this whole project move
even faster than we ever really

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.110
had expected.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.570
And we rode those waves
throughout January and February

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.650
as we opened up to

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.330
younger people as we opened
up to new eligibility.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:03.660
And we got something for
Alaska, some major traffic,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.830
we'd see 20, 40,000
pages on a given day.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.990
And for this scraping
based website

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.880
that has no backup and no
team besides myself, my boss,

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.420
we got a million page views.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:19.410
We connected the thousands of
Alaskans to their appointments,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.190
and it served as the Alaska
statewide vaccine finder tool

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.590
for many months.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.290
And on a personal note, I got
my very first piece of fan mail

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.160
for an R project, and I
definitely sent this to my boss

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.370
as soon as I got that email.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.438
But as we moved from out
of those really crazy days

00:09:38.438 --> 00:09:40.230
of the early pandemic,
we want to turn this

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:41.600
into something more stable.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.740
I'd spend enough time trying
to fix a scraper at 9 PM or 6

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.240
a.m., and one of
the big changes was

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:50.350
that we started with
one major data source.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.573
And that's scraping this
system called Prepmod.

00:09:53.573 --> 00:09:54.990
But all of a sudden
we're bringing

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:56.280
in all sorts of other data.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.430
We've got pharmacy APIs, we've
got some open source project

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.200
that brings in pharmacy chains.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.240
Vaccines.gov came
on board and we

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.850
access some of that CDC data.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.250
And then all of a sudden
this simple project

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:09.990
turns into kind of
a complex project

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:13.140
with six different opportunities
for things to break.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.480
And what I was
definitely overdue to do

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.870
was to turn all these into
modules so that when something

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.318
breaks, you can turn it off.

00:10:20.318 --> 00:10:22.110
When something needs
to be troubleshooted,

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.990
you can target that in
its own individual file,

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.220
in its own individual function.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.820
And it made it from
a brittle website

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.060
into a less brittle website.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.750
I also tried to bring
in more error handling

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.550
errors would break the website
in the early days for sure.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.160
So I brought in a bunch
more try catch statements.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.610
So that you can run a statement
and if it successfully

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:46.480
executes you get
your data back.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.270
But if and when
that error comes,

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.530
we can still return
some valid data.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.630
We can still keep the rest
of the sources online.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.270
And we don't break
the system just

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.880
because those errors did come.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.440
For automation,

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.390
we really wanted to
track appointments

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.130
24/7 because they were really
going fast in those early days.

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:06.047
And so we wanted to
scrape every single minute

00:11:06.047 --> 00:11:09.440
to see how close we could keep
up with vaccine appointments.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.880
And the way I did this was
with a Heroku R Buildpack

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.270
and a Heroku Cron
job where I basically

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:19.900
ran one script every 10 minutes
and then in those 10 minutes

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.260
I scraped several times.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.582
So seven or 10 times every
10 minutes we scraped

00:11:24.582 --> 00:11:26.290
and we scraped and we
scraped and scraped

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.207
and about 5 million
HTML pages, I

00:11:28.207 --> 00:11:30.040
think we scraped before
we finally moved off

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.235
of that, that scraper.

00:11:31.235 --> 00:11:32.860
So that prototype
turned into something

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:36.400
that got used quite a bit.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.518
We also had to adapt to
the complexity that came up

00:11:39.518 --> 00:11:40.810
throughout the process of this.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.860
If you may remember,
there is a lot of phases.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.600
There's phase one b, tier
one, phase one, a tier three,

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.850
and we got a lot of
requests to have information

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:52.000
like this on the website, and we
don't like too much complexity

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.210
and we're pretty militant
about having simple information

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:56.740
as much as possible.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:59.320
So the way that we did this
on the front end was we

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:01.660
kept the appointments
above the fold,

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:03.280
as they say, so
appointments up high,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.300
but we radically simplified
the eligibility information

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.520
and kind of had it
slightly hidden.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.520
But we kept the scope of this
as narrow as we can to, here's

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.660
where you find
your appointments.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.100
And then more complexity
came in that all of a sudden,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.700
we've got a lot of vaccine
appointments and supplies

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.360
at a certain point, and it's
turning into a big list that

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.540
needs some management.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.845
And because we don't want
to add complicated menus

00:12:26.845 --> 00:12:28.220
and we wanted this
to work really

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.590
well on phones,
what we did was just

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:33.200
little, little HTML
buttons that allows

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.875
you to search this table
for the city or region.

00:12:35.875 --> 00:12:37.250
And there's a lot
of string a lot

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.300
of string searching
going on in this.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:43.400
So that was our strategy for
dealing with that complexity.

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:45.920
So what have I learned
throughout this experience?

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.800
One is that there is a really
subtle power to creating

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.440
an MVP, to creating a demo.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.030
So if we had asked in advance
permission to do this website,

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.890
we would have encountered
a lot of reasons to say no.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.500
It's like, what's
your budget, what's

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.300
the plan, what's
your backup plan?

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.058
Who's the staff
in charge of this?

00:13:05.058 --> 00:13:07.100
But when you put something
in the hands of people

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.490
where they can interact
with it and break

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.160
through all those barriers,
there's really a lot of magic

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:13.860
to it.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.718
And these prototypes,
in our case,

00:13:15.718 --> 00:13:17.010
they're not they're not pretty.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.790
They're text on a phone.
But sometimes a prototype,

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.360
just a proof of
concept, can sometimes

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:26.000
blow people's minds just enough
to break through and make

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.270
progress on projects.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.260
There's also some

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:32.840
secret value in building
your own data set.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.570
And in this case, we were
really the only people

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.480
scraping and
aggregating appointments

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.040
across the state of Alaska.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:43.190
And because we wanted to track
how many appointments there

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:46.050
were, we had the
tools to do that.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:48.020
And I displayed how
many appointments

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:50.487
were available at any given
moment throughout the day.

00:13:50.487 --> 00:13:52.070
And this is basically
out of curiosity

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:54.170
because we wanted to see
how fast things were going.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.940
But at some point we learned
that the state health

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:57.773
department was actually
watching this number

00:13:57.773 --> 00:14:00.813
and actually using that
number to make decisions

00:14:00.813 --> 00:14:02.480
on when it's time to
open up eligibility

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:04.890
or when it's time to
make some changes.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.550
So when you build that data set,
sometimes you have the ability

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:11.780
to find value and find
unexpected value in places that

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.300
you didn't

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:15.330
intend.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:18.360
So R is a great
prototyping tool,

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:23.580
especially with all of
you who love R and have

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.050
spent some time with it.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.270
This project was a
series of data frames

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:31.950
that basically would be created,
updated, modified, and then

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:32.500
destroyed

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.620
every every single minute
is what we would do.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:37.230
And because I'm comfortable
manipulating data frames,

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:39.060
that's what I did.

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:43.450
And it's ultimately this data
frame that took a lot of work.

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:45.880
But that data frame
turns into the product.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:50.040
It's the world's simplest
database that is JSON or CSV,

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.360
but that turns into
the actual product.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.770
So if you have that
ability to work fast in R,

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.650
you're really set up well
to create prototypes.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:04.040
And in some projects,
if this project

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.320
had a different
timeline and we knew

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.660
we had months to
work on this, I may

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.880
have used something more
traditional like Node.js

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.760
or Python or something
more standard to build out

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.400
a web service and a website
and maybe some React

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:17.070
or something.

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.730
But this is a project
where the timeline was now,

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:23.270
and it's a timeline of the
prototype is now the product.

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.810
So in those projects where speed
and flexibility matter most,

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:29.940
I think R is absolutely
your superpower.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:34.850
So if you've put in the reps and
have that ability to work fast,

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.600
you're in a really
great place to do that.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:37.800
If you haven't put in the
reps, you're doing it.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:38.900
You're doing it right now.

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:42.110
And I think that you
all should appreciate

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:46.010
that you have the skills,
the abilities and absolutely

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:50.570
the tools to really quickly
make custom software or custom

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:52.580
analyses or models.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:54.830
And your ability to
do that fast, I think,

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:57.980
can sometimes be a huge
advantage in projects.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:01.100
So if a computer is like
a bicycle for the mind,

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.050
I have to think of R as like
the electric cargo bike.

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.330
It's something where you
can help a lot of people.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.000
You can do absolutely
anything with it

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.470
and you can go fast and
really have a lot of fun.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.850
So thank you very much.

